  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-77535",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Using $m∠B=m∠C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & =m∠C \\\\ z+{10}^{∘} & =2z \\\\ z & ={10}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $z={10}^{∘}$ in the expression for $m∠C,$ we get\n\n$$\nm∠C=2({10}^{∘})={20}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m∠A$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+2m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+2\\cdot {20}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠A & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
